1
00:02:22,105 --> 00:02:28,105
A tornado is 100 mph. A hurricane is 150. This is something else!

2
00:04:44,210 --> 00:04:50,210
500 MPH Storm is a action, science fiction, tv
movie film directed by Daniel Lusko.

3
00:07:06,315 --> 00:07:12,315
When an energy experiment goes haywire, a rash of
massive hurricanes rips across North America.

4
00:09:28,421 --> 00:09:34,421
A high school science teacher must get his family to safety before the hurricanes
merge, creating a "hypercane" with the power to wipe the US off the map.

5
00:11:50,526 --> 00:11:56,526
Nathan Sims, played by Casper Van Dien.

6
00:14:12,631 --> 00:14:18,631
Mona Sims, played by Sarah Lieving.

7
00:16:34,736 --> 00:16:40,736
Simon Caprisi, played by Michael Beach.

8
00:18:56,842 --> 00:19:02,842
Captain Wright, played by Keith Meriweather.

9
00:21:18,947 --> 00:21:24,947
Corporal Gentile, played by Alex Knight.

10
00:23:41,052 --> 00:23:47,052
Tech Oxenberg, played by Dale O´Malley.

11
00:26:03,157 --> 00:26:09,157
The 2013 El Reno tornado was an extremely large, powerful, and erratic tornado that occurred and hit
a rural part of Central Oklahoma during the early evening of Friday, May 31, 2013.

12
00:28:25,263 --> 00:28:31,263
This rain-wrapped, multiple-vortex tornado was the widest
tornado ever recorded, peaking at 2.6 miles across.

13
00:30:47,368 --> 00:30:53,368
It was part of a larger weather system that produced
dozens of tornadoes over the preceding days.

14
00:33:09,473 --> 00:33:15,473
The tornado initially formed at 6:03 p.m.

15
00:35:31,578 --> 00:35:37,578
CDT (23:03 UTC) about 8.3 miles (13.4 km) west-southwest of El Reno, rapidly growing in
size and becoming more violent as it tracked through central Canadian County.

16
00:37:53,684 --> 00:37:59,684
Remaining over mostly open terrain, the tornado did not impact many structures; however, measurements from
mobile weather radars revealed extreme winds in excess of 313 mph (504 km/h) within sub-vortices.

17
00:40:15,789 --> 00:40:21,789
These are among the highest observed wind speeds on Earth, just slightly lower
than the wind speeds of the 1999 Bridge Creek–Moore tornado.

18
00:42:37,894 --> 00:42:43,894
As it crossed U.S.

19
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:06,000
81, it had grown to a record-breaking width of 2.6 miles (4.2
km), beating the previous width record set in 2004.

20
00:47:22,105 --> 00:47:28,105
Turning northeastward, the tornado soon weakened.

21
00:49:44,210 --> 00:49:50,210
Upon crossing Interstate 40, the tornado dissipated around 6:43 p.m.

22
00:52:06,315 --> 00:52:12,315
CDT (23:43 UTC), after tracking for 16.2 miles (26.1 km).

23
00:54:28,421 --> 00:54:34,421
It avoided affecting more densely populated areas near and
within the Oklahoma City metropolitan area.

24
00:56:50,526 --> 00:56:56,526
The tornado killed four storm chasers (three professional and one
amateur), the first known deaths in the history of storm chasing.

25
00:59:12,631 --> 00:59:18,631
Although the tornado remained over mostly open terrain, dozens of storm chasers
unaware of its immense size and erratic movement were caught off-guard.

26
01:01:34,736 --> 01:01:40,736
81, TWISTEX scientist and engineer Tim Samaras, along with his son Paul
and research partner Carl Young, died in the tornado.

27
01:03:56,842 --> 01:04:02,842
Paul Samaras and Young were ejected from their Chevrolet Cobalt by the storm's
sub-vortex, while Tim was still buckled in the passenger's seat.

28
01:06:18,947 --> 01:06:24,947
Local resident Richard Henderson, who decided to follow
the storm, lost his life in that same area.

29
01:08:41,052 --> 01:08:47,052
He snapped a picture of the tornado from his
cellular phone before it struck him.

30
01:11:03,157 --> 01:11:09,157
Other chasers, including Mike Bettes of The Weather Channel and Reed
Timmer, either were injured or had their vehicles damaged.

31
01:13:25,263 --> 01:13:31,263
A Doppler on Wheels-based analysis of how the tornado impacted these teams
revealed that they were hit by an intense internal sub-vortex.

32
01:15:47,368 --> 01:15:53,368
Overall, the tornado was responsible for eight fatalities and 151 injuries.

33
01:18:09,473 --> 01:18:15,473
Due to the ferocity and sheer size, as well as its irregular movement and the deaths linked with
this tornado, it has become one of the most studied and infamous tornadoes ever.

34
01:20:31,578 --> 01:20:37,578
The National Weather Service referred to the tornado as "the most dangerous tornado in storm observing history."
Alongside rush hour traffic, thousands of
residents in Oklahoma City attempted to outrun the storm by taking to the roads in an attempt to drive out of the tornado's projected path.

35
01:22:53,684 --> 01:22:59,684
By attempting to escape the storm by vehicle, in direct contrast to the recommended plan of action, residents put themselves at great risk from
the storm; had the tornado maintained itself and passed over the congested freeways, more than 500 lives could have been lost.

36
01:25:15,789 --> 01:25:21,789
The film explores themes of experiment, tornado,
hurricane, hot air balloon, scientist.

37
01:27:37,894 --> 01:27:43,894
The tone is emotional and thought_provoking and intense.
